{"parentUuid":"ba795272-c143-433c-8a4a-a129e3cf1142","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/guygouri/.claude/projects/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/memory/ern-formal-mm02-exploration.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/.claude/projects/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/memory/ern-formal-mm02-exploration.md","content":"---\nname: ern-formal-mm02-exploration\ndescription: \"Conventions of the ERN formal/ exploratory passes (MM-02 and MM-03 branches, French briefs, reports/ never merged, spec kept outside the repo, lake path, axiom rule)\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180\n  modified: 2026-10-31T19:59:32.308Z\n---\n\nExploratory Lean passes on ERN specs arrive as pasted French task briefs.\n- MM-02 v0.2: branch `formal/memory_model_0.2`. MM-02 v0.3 (passes 1 to 4, 2026-10-28): `formal/memory_model_0.3`, module `Ern.MM02` (`formal/Ern/MM02.lean`), reports `MM02_v0.3*.md`.\n- MM-03.3 v0.2 jalon 1 (access discipline, 2026-10-29): branch `formal/access_model_0.1` cut from `formal/memory_model_0.3`, module `Ern.MM03` (`formal/Ern/MM03.lean`, imports MM02 unchanged), report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1.md`. Key finding there: Inv3 is not inductive under release, so preservation is proved under a structural invariant `WF3` (clause `Tree`).\n- MM-03.3 pass 2 (arbitrages A1-A11, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p2.md`. Code, docstrings and commit messages in English; only the report in French. The brief forbids weakening any pass-1 statement: diff statements mechanically (cut at the top-level `:=`, not the first one) and give a Lean witness for every added hypothesis or retired statement.\n- MM-03.3 pass 3 (arbitrages B1-B10, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p3.md`; `Inv3base` = pass-2 Inv3, `Inv3 = Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder`; WF3 gained RootsDisjoint, RootChain (B1) and RootHeld (A4). Briefs save to the scratchpad as `task_mm03_p<N>.md`.\n- MM-03.3 pass 4 (arbitrages C1-C9, 2026-10-29, closes milestone 1): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p4.md` with an extra section (n) « bilan de clôture ». `Inv3 = Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder ∧ NoDangling`; `alloc` takes a shape (enterFrame locals encoding); `RootsDisjoint` left `WF3`.\n- MM-03.3 pass 5 (arbitrages D1-D4, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p5.md` with an extra section (o) (domain changes). `S3.init statics`, `S3.arenas`, `Reachable k statics` with newArena/issue/reset. Open questions left for the arbiter: statics owned by statics, nested arena ownership, kind rule of `IsInternalFor` ((e) 1 to 6).\n- MM-03.3 pass 6 (arbitrages F1-F7, 2026-10-30): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p6.md` with extra section (p) (F7 progress). Arenas by holder of `internal`, `store`, `callParams` ghost field. F6 (`call_protects_params_safe`) is false as stated (counterexamples kept); F7 (a) second store accepted.\n- MM-03.3 pass 7 (arbitrages G1-G5, 2026-10-30): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p7.md` with extra sections (p) verdict changes and (q) status of NoOrphan / F6. G1 clause in EnterPre (EnterPreBase = pass-6 text), G2 leaf store with arrival clause, G3 NoOrphan proved via auxiliary RootLive, G4 AnchorOrderHeld checked in arrival states. F6 still false (ancestor, unblock by releases, exit re-anchoring); G2 guard bypassed by a helper then release/endUse/free/exit.\n- MM-03.3 pass 8 (arbitrages J1-J2, 2026-10-30, closes jalon 1 again): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p8.md`, sections (a)-(o), (p) verdict changes, (q) closing balance in four classes. J1: `SameShape` (ordered isomorphism, open question), `corr`/`mvTo` move held authorities and owned places to corresponding parts, issue to a free leaf only, `newArena` needs the arena value Init, `init_only_by_writes` over a `Step` union. Open: J1 bullets 2/3 conflict (issue into a Moved leaf of the arena value).\n- Jalon 2 pass 1 (refinement MM-03 → MM-02, arbitrages K1-K5, 2026-10-31): branch `formal/refinement_0.1` cut from `formal/access_model_0.1`, new module `Ern.MM23` (`formal/Ern/MM23.lean`, imports MM02 and MM03 unchanged), report `formal/reports/MM23_v0.1.md` sections (a)-(f). Specs now `~/Downloads/ERN_MM02_v04.md` and `~/Downloads/ERN_MM-03.3_v0.3_revisee.md`. `Repr tok L s3 s2` is decidable (structure + `decidable_of_iff`, needs `set_option synthInstance.maxSize 4096`). Axiom sweep by a Lean script walking module theorems with `collectAxioms` (grep-based sweep misses namespaced cases).\n- Spec files live in `~/Downloads/` (`ERN_Memory_model.md` = v0.2 incl. MM-03.1/03.2, `ERN_Memory_Model_v0.3_consolidee.md`, `ERN_Memory_Model_0.3.3.md` = MM-03.3 v0.2), not in `specs/`.\n\n**Why:** the briefs require French commit messages (one per step), docstrings citing `MM-0x.y` rules, statement changes justified in the report, and say `formal/reports/` is exploration never merged into `main`. Later briefs add \"arbitrages\" that answer earlier report questions.\n\n**How to apply:** start a new pass from the latest branch tip; run an adversarial review workflow over module and report before delivering; `lake` is not on PATH, call `~/.elan/bin/lake`; keep the Lake library name `Ern`. `formal/Ern/.gitkeep` keeps showing as deleted: restore it with `git checkout`, never commit the deletion. A post-commit hook can make `git commit` exceed 2 minutes: give it a long timeout. The axiom rule ({propext, Quot.sound} only) forbids core lemmas that pull `Classical.choice` (`List.getElem?_mapIdx`, `List.eq_nil_iff_forall_not_mem`, `List.nodup_range`, `List.pairwise_lt_range`): sweep `#print axioms` over every theorem and replace them with local lemmas. Mathlib tactics (`set`, `tauto`) are unavailable. Reviews keep catching report errors: quote old forms from the old commit, count declarations with a command, and base an evidence claim only on a check that would fail on the defect. After fixing review findings, run a second targeted review of the fixes: in MM-03 pass 2 a fix over-rejected legitimate programs. Before calling a clause \"missing\" from an arbitrage, re-read every earlier arbitrage still in force: in pass 3 the clause needed for exitFrame_no_dangling was A4's root-holder rule, which A1 puts in WF3 and pass 2 had left out. `timeout` does not exist on this Mac: run `lake build` directly (about 2.5 min). When an arbitrage enlarges a definition used in hypotheses (pass 4: `Inv3` gained `NoDangling`), theorems whose text is unchanged silently narrow: give them only the conjunct they use. Before retiring a counterexample whose witness breaks a new invariant clause, look for another witness: pass-4 review found one and flagged the weakening as major. Check obligations against the initial state: the base frame of `S3.init` owns no place, so an obligation about it can be vacuous on reached states. A review fix can itself pick a semantic reading (pass 5: the kind conjunct of `IsInternalFor` rejected a `newArena` that D3 leaves undecided): check every fix for verdicts it changes on cases the text is silent on. Module docstrings must cite the report's own numbering, not the review's decision numbers. Arbiter-supplied theorems can be false: check them on small reached states (owned storage, a parameter that is the ancestor of another) before trying a proof. Every factual claim in the report needs a module witness, or reviewers flag it; never say \"blocked until X\" without checking escape sequences such as ending the owner value. When a precondition gains clauses, lemmas taking it as a hypothesis narrow silently: name the old precondition (pass 7: `EnterPreBase`) and give the lemmas only what they use. A guard on one operation (G2 on `store`) is usually bypassable through another (a helper authority then `release`/`endUse`/`free`/exit): look for such bypasses before claiming a configuration is excluded. A new check on an operation with a defined failure (`issue` → `fail`) must not turn that failure into `reject`. Use one adversarial reviewer per arbitrage lens; each found distinct real issues in pass 7. When a restriction rejects an old counterexample, try to rebuild a witness that satisfies the new clause before restating it as a rejection (pass 8: a same-shape destination restored `transfer_not_inv3_preserving`). Drop hypotheses a proof does not use. When cases disappear because their state is no longer reached, replay their verdicts on a new reachable state and list the drops. Machine-check every changed verdict the report lists, including the old witnesses' rejected steps. Never state \"only clause X fails\" as `X → Pre` next to `¬ X`: that is vacuous; name the other clauses (pass 8: `TransferPreBase` with an iff) and state them. Run a second targeted check after fixing verification findings: in pass 8 it caught that vacuous form. `omega` proving a conjunction goal pulls `Classical.choice`: split the goal first. `meta` is a keyword in this Lean: never name a hypothesis `meta`. `set` (Mathlib) is unavailable: use `obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ : ∃ x, x = e := ⟨_, rfl⟩`. `if_pos`/`if_neg`/`ite_cond_eq_true` are deprecated: use `simp only [h, ↓reduceIte]`. When `S3.Usable.mono` is applied with `rfl` for `hP`, give `(s := …) (s' := …)` explicitly or it unifies the wrong way.\n","numLines":27,"startLine":1,"totalLines":27}},"displayPath":"../../../../.claude/projects/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/memory/ern-formal-mm02-exploration.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c059319e-5dfc-4732-bc01-3d6c723d701a","timestamp":"2026-10-31T22:24:19.808Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/guygouri/.claude/projects/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/memory/ern-formal-mm02-exploration.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t---\n2\tname: ern-formal-mm02-exploration\n3\tdescription: \"Conventions of the ERN formal/ exploratory passes (MM-02 and MM-03 branches, French briefs, reports/ never merged, spec kept outside the repo, lake path, axiom rule)\"\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180\n8\t  modified: 2026-10-31T19:59:32.308Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tExploratory Lean passes on ERN specs arrive as pasted French task briefs.\n12\t- MM-02 v0.2: branch `formal/memory_model_0.2`. MM-02 v0.3 (passes 1 to 4, 2026-10-28): `formal/memory_model_0.3`, module `Ern.MM02` (`formal/Ern/MM02.lean`), reports `MM02_v0.3*.md`.\n13\t- MM-03.3 v0.2 jalon 1 (access discipline, 2026-10-29): branch `formal/access_model_0.1` cut from `formal/memory_model_0.3`, module `Ern.MM03` (`formal/Ern/MM03.lean`, imports MM02 unchanged), report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1.md`. Key finding there: Inv3 is not inductive under release, so preservation is proved under a structural invariant `WF3` (clause `Tree`).\n14\t- MM-03.3 pass 2 (arbitrages A1-A11, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p2.md`. Code, docstrings and commit messages in English; only the report in French. The brief forbids weakening any pass-1 statement: diff statements mechanically (cut at the top-level `:=`, not the first one) and give a Lean witness for every added hypothesis or retired statement.\n15\t- MM-03.3 pass 3 (arbitrages B1-B10, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p3.md`; `Inv3base` = pass-2 Inv3, `Inv3 = Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder`; WF3 gained RootsDisjoint, RootChain (B1) and RootHeld (A4). Briefs save to the scratchpad as `task_mm03_p<N>.md`.\n16\t- MM-03.3 pass 4 (arbitrages C1-C9, 2026-10-29, closes milestone 1): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p4.md` with an extra section (n) « bilan de clôture ». `Inv3 = Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder ∧ NoDangling`; `alloc` takes a shape (enterFrame locals encoding); `RootsDisjoint` left `WF3`.\n17\t- MM-03.3 pass 5 (arbitrages D1-D4, 2026-10-29): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p5.md` with an extra section (o) (domain changes). `S3.init statics`, `S3.arenas`, `Reachable k statics` with newArena/issue/reset. Open questions left for the arbiter: statics owned by statics, nested arena ownership, kind rule of `IsInternalFor` ((e) 1 to 6).\n18\t- MM-03.3 pass 6 (arbitrages F1-F7, 2026-10-30): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p6.md` with extra section (p) (F7 progress). Arenas by holder of `internal`, `store`, `callParams` ghost field. F6 (`call_protects_params_safe`) is false as stated (counterexamples kept); F7 (a) second store accepted.\n19\t- MM-03.3 pass 7 (arbitrages G1-G5, 2026-10-30): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p7.md` with extra sections (p) verdict changes and (q) status of NoOrphan / F6. G1 clause in EnterPre (EnterPreBase = pass-6 text), G2 leaf store with arrival clause, G3 NoOrphan proved via auxiliary RootLive, G4 AnchorOrderHeld checked in arrival states. F6 still false (ancestor, unblock by releases, exit re-anchoring); G2 guard bypassed by a helper then release/endUse/free/exit.\n20\t- MM-03.3 pass 8 (arbitrages J1-J2, 2026-10-30, closes jalon 1 again): same branch, report `formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p8.md`, sections (a)-(o), (p) verdict changes, (q) closing balance in four classes. J1: `SameShape` (ordered isomorphism, open question), `corr`/`mvTo` move held authorities and owned places to corresponding parts, issue to a free leaf only, `newArena` needs the arena value Init, `init_only_by_writes` over a `Step` union. Open: J1 bullets 2/3 conflict (issue into a Moved leaf of the arena value).\n21\t- Jalon 2 pass 1 (refinement MM-03 → MM-02, arbitrages K1-K5, 2026-10-31): branch `formal/refinement_0.1` cut from `formal/access_model_0.1`, new module `Ern.MM23` (`formal/Ern/MM23.lean`, imports MM02 and MM03 unchanged), report `formal/reports/MM23_v0.1.md` sections (a)-(f). Specs now `~/Downloads/ERN_MM02_v04.md` and `~/Downloads/ERN_MM-03.3_v0.3_revisee.md`. `Repr tok L s3 s2` is decidable (structure + `decidable_of_iff`, needs `set_option synthInstance.maxSize 4096`). Axiom sweep by a Lean script walking module theorems with `collectAxioms` (grep-based sweep misses namespaced cases).\n22\t- Spec files live in `~/Downloads/` (`ERN_Memory_model.md` = v0.2 incl. MM-03.1/03.2, `ERN_Memory_Model_v0.3_consolidee.md`, `ERN_Memory_Model_0.3.3.md` = MM-03.3 v0.2), not in `specs/`.\n23\t\n24\t**Why:** the briefs require French commit messages (one per step), docstrings citing `MM-0x.y` rules, statement changes justified in the report, and say `formal/reports/` is exploration never merged into `main`. Later briefs add \"arbitrages\" that answer earlier report questions.\n25\t\n26\t**How to apply:** start a new pass from the latest branch tip; run an adversarial review workflow over module and report before delivering; `lake` is not on PATH, call `~/.elan/bin/lake`; keep the Lake library name `Ern`. `formal/Ern/.gitkeep` keeps showing as deleted: restore it with `git checkout`, never commit the deletion. A post-commit hook can make `git commit` exceed 2 minutes: give it a long timeout. The axiom rule ({propext, Quot.sound} only) forbids core lemmas that pull `Classical.choice` (`List.getElem?_mapIdx`, `List.eq_nil_iff_forall_not_mem`, `List.nodup_range`, `List.pairwise_lt_range`): sweep `#print axioms` over every theorem and replace them with local lemmas. Mathlib tactics (`set`, `tauto`) are unavailable. Reviews keep catching report errors: quote old forms from the old commit, count declarations with a command, and base an evidence claim only on a check that would fail on the defect. After fixing review findings, run a second targeted review of the fixes: in MM-03 pass 2 a fix over-rejected legitimate programs. Before calling a clause \"missing\" from an arbitrage, re-read every earlier arbitrage still in force: in pass 3 the clause needed for exitFrame_no_dangling was A4's root-holder rule, which A1 puts in WF3 and pass 2 had left out. `timeout` does not exist on this Mac: run `lake build` directly (about 2.5 min). When an arbitrage enlarges a definition used in hypotheses (pass 4: `Inv3` gained `NoDangling`), theorems whose text is unchanged silently narrow: give them only the conjunct they use. Before retiring a counterexample whose witness breaks a new invariant clause, look for another witness: pass-4 review found one and flagged the weakening as major. Check obligations against the initial state: the base frame of `S3.init` owns no place, so an obligation about it can be vacuous on reached states. A review fix can itself pick a semantic reading (pass 5: the kind conjunct of `IsInternalFor` rejected a `newArena` that D3 leaves undecided): check every fix for verdicts it changes on cases the text is silent on. Module docstrings must cite the report's own numbering, not the review's decision numbers. Arbiter-supplied theorems can be false: check them on small reached states (owned storage, a parameter that is the ancestor of another) before trying a proof. Every factual claim in the report needs a module witness, or reviewers flag it; never say \"blocked until X\" without checking escape sequences such as ending the owner value. When a precondition gains clauses, lemmas taking it as a hypothesis narrow silently: name the old precondition (pass 7: `EnterPreBase`) and give the lemmas only what they use. A guard on one operation (G2 on `store`) is usually bypassable through another (a helper authority then `release`/`endUse`/`free`/exit): look for such bypasses before claiming a configuration is excluded. A new check on an operation with a defined failure (`issue` → `fail`) must not turn that failure into `reject`. Use one adversarial reviewer per arbitrage lens; each found distinct real issues in pass 7. When a restriction rejects an old counterexample, try to rebuild a witness that satisfies the new clause before restating it as a rejection (pass 8: a same-shape destination restored `transfer_not_inv3_preserving`). Drop hypotheses a proof does not use. When cases disappear because their state is no longer reached, replay their verdicts on a new reachable state and list the drops. Machine-check every changed verdict the report lists, including the old witnesses' rejected steps. Never state \"only clause X fails\" as `X → Pre` next to `¬ X`: that is vacuous; name the other clauses (pass 8: `TransferPreBase` with an iff) and state them. Run a second targeted check after fixing verification findings: in pass 8 it caught that vacuous form. `omega` proving a conjunction goal pulls `Classical.choice`: split the goal first. `meta` is a keyword in this Lean: never name a hypothesis `meta`. `set` (Mathlib) is unavailable: use `obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ : ∃ x, x = e := ⟨_, rfl⟩`. `if_pos`/`if_neg`/`ite_cond_eq_true` are deprecated: use `simp only [h, ↓reduceIte]`. When `S3.Usable.mono` is applied with `rfl` for `hP`, give `(s := …) (s' := …)` explicitly or it unifies the wrong way.\n27\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/formal","sessionId":"c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"formal/refinement_0.1","slug":"effervescent-gathering-wind"}
{"parentUuid":"c059319e-5dfc-4732-bc01-3d6c723d701a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/task_mm23_p1.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/task_mm23_p1.md","content":"# Tâche : jalon 2, passe 1 — raffinement MM-03 → MM-02 (disposition, relation de représentation, accès, allocation, cadres)\n\n## Git\nNouvelle branche brouillon `formal/refinement_0.1`, créée depuis `formal/access_model_0.1`. Pas de merge, pas de PR. Tout sous `formal/`. `Ern/MM02.lean` et `Ern/MM03.lean` inchangés : un lemme qui leur manque est ajouté dans le nouveau module. Nouveau module `Ern/MM23.lean`, qui importe les deux.\nSpécifications jointes, non copiées : `ERN_MM02_v04.md` (MM-01 et MM-02 v0.4, validées) et MM-03.3 v0.3 révisée. Elles font foi ; les deux modules existants sont des brouillons. Tout écart entre un module et son texte est rapporté, jamais corrigé silencieusement.\n\n## Langue\nCode, docstrings, commentaires, messages de commit en anglais. Rapport en français.\n\n## Arbitrages (font foi)\nK1. Disposition paramétrique. `Layout` associe à chaque feuille une plage d'octets `(allocation, offset, size)` d'une allocation MM-02 ; elle ne fixe ni ABI, ni alignement de type, ni ordre des champs. Obligations `LayoutWF` : (a) les feuilles d'un même emplacement racine sont dans la même allocation ; (b) deux feuilles distinctes ont des plages disjointes ; (c) chaque plage est incluse dans son allocation ; (d) une forme à une feuille peut avoir une taille nulle. Aucune autre hypothèse.\nK2. Racines et allocations.\n  - Racine locale d'un cadre ou statique ↔ allocation automatique ou statique ; son autorité de désallocation est un jeton propre au cadre, compatible par construction.\n  - Racine créée par `alloc` ↔ allocation créée par `AllocStep`.\n  - Partie ↔ sous-plage de l'allocation de sa racine ; bloc d'arène ↔ sous-plage de l'allocation du stockage de l'arène, sans allocation propre.\n  - Le choix de la base d'une nouvelle allocation est un paramètre `Placement`, dont l'obligation est `AllocPre` de MM-02 (fraîcheur, alignement, non-chevauchement des allocations vivantes) ; `K.Satisfiable` implique l'existence d'un placement valide.\nK3. Relation de représentation `Repr k L s3 s2` : `LayoutWF` ; toute racine utilisable a son allocation vivante ; toute feuille `Init` a tous ses octets initialisés dans MM-02 ; une racine morte par `free` ou par sortie de cadre a son allocation morte ; une racine morte par `endUse` (stockage possédé non libéré) garde son allocation vivante. État initial : `Repr` entre `S3.init statics` et l'état MM-02 obtenu depuis `State.empty` par les `AllocStep` des statiques, puis l'écriture des statiques déclarées `Init`.\nK4. Correspondance des pas, périmètre de la passe 1 :\n  - `read3` ↦ `ReadStep` sur chaque plage lue, accès de provenance = allocation de la racine, autorité `read` ;\n  - `write3`, `writeShared` ↦ `WriteStep`, autorité `write` ;\n  - `alloc` `ok` ↦ `AllocStep` ; `alloc` `fail` ↦ aucun pas ;\n  - `free` ↦ `DeallocStep`, autorité `Compatible` issue de `FreeCompat` ;\n  - `enterFrame` ↦ un `AllocStep` par racine locale ; `exitFrame` ↦ un `DeallocStep` par allocation automatique du cadre, et aucun pour le stockage possédé qui meurt avec lui (fuite si non libéré) ;\n  - `derive`, `split`, `release`, `depend`, `endUse`, `pin`, `unpin`, `newArena`, `reset` ↦ aucun pas.\n  `transfer`, `store` et `issue` sont hors de cette passe.\nK5. Obligations opaques de MM-02 : `stat` (concurrence) est vrai dans le fragment séquentiel ; `dec` (alignement d'accès, validité de représentation) est une hypothèse paramétrique explicite, jamais instanciée par `True` sans le signaler.\n\n## Étapes (un commit par étape)\n1. K1–K3 : `Layout`, `LayoutWF`, `Placement`, `Repr`, `repr_init`.\n2. R1 : `read3` `ok` et `Repr` ⇒ `ReadStep` `ok` sur chaque plage, jamais `ub`, et la valeur lue est celle des octets. R2 : `write3`, `writeShared` `ok` et `Repr` ⇒ `WriteStep` `ok`, et `Repr` préservée.\n3. R6 (périmètre K4) : pour chaque opération, `ok` en MM-03 et `Repr` ⇒ la séquence MM-02 associée n'a que des issues `ok`, et les états d'arrivée sont en `Repr` ; `alloc` `fail` ⇒ aucun pas, `Repr` préservée.\n4. R4 : sur un état en `Repr` avec un état atteint de MM-03, aucune autorité active ne couvre une feuille dont l'allocation MM-02 est morte (depuis `NoDangling`).\n5. `exclusive_bytes` : sous `Repr` et I1, si une autorité a le mode effectif `Modify` sur une feuille, aucune autre autorité active qui n'en descend pas n'a de mode effectif sur une feuille dont la plage chevauche la sienne. Justification mémoire du non-aliasing, côté noyau seulement.\n6. Cas : un témoin par opération du périmètre, depuis `S3.init`, avec l'état MM-02 en `Repr` et la séquence MM-02 exhibée.\n\n## Hors périmètre (passes suivantes)\n`transfer`, `store`, `issue` (octets de pointeur, provenance) ; `DDSound` réel depuis `LayoutWF` et `DeclaredDisjoint` ; stabilité des adresses épinglées ; concurrence ; traduction bas niveau.\n\n## Règles\nZéro `sorry` ; `#print axioms` au plus {propext, Quot.sound} ; aucun `noncomputable` ; `lake build` sans avertissement ; docstrings citant la clause (MM-02 v0.4, MM-03.3 v0.3) ou l'arbitrage K ; rien d'inventé ; aucun énoncé affaibli ; revue adverse centrée sur `Repr` (une relation trop faible rendrait R1, R2 et R6 vides : chaque théorème doit avoir un témoin où sa conclusion n'est pas triviale) ; contre-exemples conservés.\n\n## Livrable\nModule commité ; `formal/reports/MM23_v0.1.md` : (a) clause → déclaration ; (b) statut de R1, R2, R4, R6 et de `exclusive_bytes` ; (c) décisions non dictées par les textes ; (d) ce qui n'est pas prouvé, et pourquoi ; (e) questions pour arbitrage ; (f) écarts entre les brouillons `MM02`/`MM03` et leurs textes, trouvés en route.\n\nSpec files: /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_MM02_v04.md, /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_MM-03.3_v0.3_revisee.md\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}},"displayPath":"../../../../../../private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/task_mm23_p1.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"888b35a6-4a2e-42b7-b4c5-c91cb4fd8642","timestamp":"2026-10-31T22:24:19.808Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/task_mm23_p1.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Tâche : jalon 2, passe 1 — raffinement MM-03 → MM-02 (disposition, relation de représentation, accès, allocation, cadres)\n2\t\n3\t## Git\n4\tNouvelle branche brouillon `formal/refinement_0.1`, créée depuis `formal/access_model_0.1`. Pas de merge, pas de PR. Tout sous `formal/`. `Ern/MM02.lean` et `Ern/MM03.lean` inchangés : un lemme qui leur manque est ajouté dans le nouveau module. Nouveau module `Ern/MM23.lean`, qui importe les deux.\n5\tSpécifications jointes, non copiées : `ERN_MM02_v04.md` (MM-01 et MM-02 v0.4, validées) et MM-03.3 v0.3 révisée. Elles font foi ; les deux modules existants sont des brouillons. Tout écart entre un module et son texte est rapporté, jamais corrigé silencieusement.\n6\t\n7\t## Langue\n8\tCode, docstrings, commentaires, messages de commit en anglais. Rapport en français.\n9\t\n10\t## Arbitrages (font foi)\n11\tK1. Disposition paramétrique. `Layout` associe à chaque feuille une plage d'octets `(allocation, offset, size)` d'une allocation MM-02 ; elle ne fixe ni ABI, ni alignement de type, ni ordre des champs. Obligations `LayoutWF` : (a) les feuilles d'un même emplacement racine sont dans la même allocation ; (b) deux feuilles distinctes ont des plages disjointes ; (c) chaque plage est incluse dans son allocation ; (d) une forme à une feuille peut avoir une taille nulle. Aucune autre hypothèse.\n12\tK2. Racines et allocations.\n13\t  - Racine locale d'un cadre ou statique ↔ allocation automatique ou statique ; son autorité de désallocation est un jeton propre au cadre, compatible par construction.\n14\t  - Racine créée par `alloc` ↔ allocation créée par `AllocStep`.\n15\t  - Partie ↔ sous-plage de l'allocation de sa racine ; bloc d'arène ↔ sous-plage de l'allocation du stockage de l'arène, sans allocation propre.\n16\t  - Le choix de la base d'une nouvelle allocation est un paramètre `Placement`, dont l'obligation est `AllocPre` de MM-02 (fraîcheur, alignement, non-chevauchement des allocations vivantes) ; `K.Satisfiable` implique l'existence d'un placement valide.\n17\tK3. Relation de représentation `Repr k L s3 s2` : `LayoutWF` ; toute racine utilisable a son allocation vivante ; toute feuille `Init` a tous ses octets initialisés dans MM-02 ; une racine morte par `free` ou par sortie de cadre a son allocation morte ; une racine morte par `endUse` (stockage possédé non libéré) garde son allocation vivante. État initial : `Repr` entre `S3.init statics` et l'état MM-02 obtenu depuis `State.empty` par les `AllocStep` des statiques, puis l'écriture des statiques déclarées `Init`.\n18\tK4. Correspondance des pas, périmètre de la passe 1 :\n19\t  - `read3` ↦ `ReadStep` sur chaque plage lue, accès de provenance = allocation de la racine, autorité `read` ;\n20\t  - `write3`, `writeShared` ↦ `WriteStep`, autorité `write` ;\n21\t  - `alloc` `ok` ↦ `AllocStep` ; `alloc` `fail` ↦ aucun pas ;\n22\t  - `free` ↦ `DeallocStep`, autorité `Compatible` issue de `FreeCompat` ;\n23\t  - `enterFrame` ↦ un `AllocStep` par racine locale ; `exitFrame` ↦ un `DeallocStep` par allocation automatique du cadre, et aucun pour le stockage possédé qui meurt avec lui (fuite si non libéré) ;\n24\t  - `derive`, `split`, `release`, `depend`, `endUse`, `pin`, `unpin`, `newArena`, `reset` ↦ aucun pas.\n25\t  `transfer`, `store` et `issue` sont hors de cette passe.\n26\tK5. Obligations opaques de MM-02 : `stat` (concurrence) est vrai dans le fragment séquentiel ; `dec` (alignement d'accès, validité de représentation) est une hypothèse paramétrique explicite, jamais instanciée par `True` sans le signaler.\n27\t\n28\t## Étapes (un commit par étape)\n29\t1. K1–K3 : `Layout`, `LayoutWF`, `Placement`, `Repr`, `repr_init`.\n30\t2. R1 : `read3` `ok` et `Repr` ⇒ `ReadStep` `ok` sur chaque plage, jamais `ub`, et la valeur lue est celle des octets. R2 : `write3`, `writeShared` `ok` et `Repr` ⇒ `WriteStep` `ok`, et `Repr` préservée.\n31\t3. R6 (périmètre K4) : pour chaque opération, `ok` en MM-03 et `Repr` ⇒ la séquence MM-02 associée n'a que des issues `ok`, et les états d'arrivée sont en `Repr` ; `alloc` `fail` ⇒ aucun pas, `Repr` préservée.\n32\t4. R4 : sur un état en `Repr` avec un état atteint de MM-03, aucune autorité active ne couvre une feuille dont l'allocation MM-02 est morte (depuis `NoDangling`).\n33\t5. `exclusive_bytes` : sous `Repr` et I1, si une autorité a le mode effectif `Modify` sur une feuille, aucune autre autorité active qui n'en descend pas n'a de mode effectif sur une feuille dont la plage chevauche la sienne. Justification mémoire du non-aliasing, côté noyau seulement.\n34\t6. Cas : un témoin par opération du périmètre, depuis `S3.init`, avec l'état MM-02 en `Repr` et la séquence MM-02 exhibée.\n35\t\n36\t## Hors périmètre (passes suivantes)\n37\t`transfer`, `store`, `issue` (octets de pointeur, provenance) ; `DDSound` réel depuis `LayoutWF` et `DeclaredDisjoint` ; stabilité des adresses épinglées ; concurrence ; traduction bas niveau.\n38\t\n39\t## Règles\n40\tZéro `sorry` ; `#print axioms` au plus {propext, Quot.sound} ; aucun `noncomputable` ; `lake build` sans avertissement ; docstrings citant la clause (MM-02 v0.4, MM-03.3 v0.3) ou l'arbitrage K ; rien d'inventé ; aucun énoncé affaibli ; revue adverse centrée sur `Repr` (une relation trop faible rendrait R1, R2 et R6 vides : chaque théorème doit avoir un témoin où sa conclusion n'est pas triviale) ; contre-exemples conservés.\n41\t\n42\t## Livrable\n43\tModule commité ; `formal/reports/MM23_v0.1.md` : (a) clause → déclaration ; (b) statut de R1, R2, R4, R6 et de `exclusive_bytes` ; (c) décisions non dictées par les textes ; (d) ce qui n'est pas prouvé, et pourquoi ; (e) questions pour arbitrage ; (f) écarts entre les brouillons `MM02`/`MM03` et leurs textes, trouvés en route.\n44\t\n45\tSpec files: /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_MM02_v04.md, /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_MM-03.3_v0.3_revisee.md\n46\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/formal","sessionId":"c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"formal/refinement_0.1","slug":"effervescent-gathering-wind"}
{"parentUuid":"888b35a6-4a2e-42b7-b4c5-c91cb4fd8642","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"ub-mode","path":"userSettings:ub-mode","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/guygouri/.claude/skills/ub-mode\n\n# ub-mode\n\nEvery time you are asked to make a change follow:\n* First Make it Work.\n* Then Make it Right.\n* Follow Practice\n\n# First Make it Work\n\n## Laziness Protocol\n\n* One decision lives in one place.\n* Ship the smallest edit that solves the problem.\n\n## Foundational Thinking\n\n* Get the data shape right before you write the logic.\n* Do first whatever helps every later phase.\n* If a concurrent writer would matter, do not share state.\n\n## TDD\n\n* Write the failing test before you change the code.\n* See it fail for the reason you expect.\n* Make the smallest change that makes it pass.\n* If an honest test is impractical, do not write a bad one.\n\n## Redesign From First Principles\n\n* Do not bolt a new requirement onto the old design.\n* Ask what you would have built if it had been there from the start, think that through, and deliver it in pieces.\n\n## Attack the Premise\n\n* When two fixes that share one premise fail the same gate, write that premise in one sentence and count which actors hold the imbalance.\n* Remove the assignment.\n* Do not start another fix until you have done that.\n\n## Subtract Before You Add\n\n* Take complexity out before you add anything.\n\n## Outcome-Oriented Execution\n\n* Aim at the end state you can check.\n* A break in the middle is allowed when you planned it and can undo it.\n\n## Build the Lever\n\n* If the work is not obvious at a glance, build the tool that does it.\n* Do one unit by hand, then make the tool match that unit.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Model the Domain\n\n* Put the domain in a structure that makes the illegal states impossible.\n* Maintain a glossary of domain terms.\n* If the code is already clear and local, leave it.\n\n### Boundary Discipline\n\n* Check the data where it enters.\n* Inside, trust it.\n* Keep the business logic in a pure function.\n\n### Type System Discipline\n\n* Make the illegal value impossible to write.\n* Parse outside data once, where it comes in.\n* Do not silence the compiler.\n\n### Make Operations Idempotent\n\n* Running it twice, or running it after a crash halfway through, ends in the same state.\n\n### Separate Before Serializing Shared State\n\n* If they do not need the same mutable thing, give each its own.\n* If they do, allow one writer, and enforce that in the structure.\n\n# Then Make it Right\n\n## Fix Root Causes\n\n* Reproduce it.\n* Fix the cause.\n* A guard that only hides the crash is not a fix.\n\n## Sequence work into verifiable units\n\n* Finish one small unit and check it.\n* Then start the next.\n\n## Test Behavior, Not Implementation\n\n* The test calls the code the way its user does and checks the result that user would see.\n* If the test would still pass when every function returned nothing, throw it out.\n\n## Crapper\n\n* Use the crapper tool\n* Reduce each function under  CRAP 10 by splitting it, by testing it, or both.\n* Consider whether case statements are worth reducing.\n\n## Dryer\n\n* Use the dryer tool to eliminate duplication that rank 0.8 or above.\n\n## Mutator\n\n* Use the mutator tool\n* Cover the uncovered.\n* Kill the survivors.\n* Delete dead code.\n\n## Refactoring\n\n* Pin what the code does.\n* Change the structure.\n* Prove the behavior did not change.\n\n# Practice\n\n## Keep a Log\n\n* Keep a log of every action you take and the reason you took it.\n* Report that log upon completion.\n\n## Guard the Context Window\n\n* Keep the main thread small.\n* Send the bulk elsewhere, and keep the summary.\n\n## Worktree Cleanup\n\n* Find what is safe to remove, confirm nothing still in use needs it, then reclaim the disk.\n* Uncommitted work waits for a decision.\n\n## Commit and Push\n\n* Commit often, in small ordered commits.\n* Each commit says why, what changed, and how you proved it.\n* Push to the main line at the end.\n"},{"name":"workflow-authoring","path":"bundled:workflow-authoring","content":"# Workflow authoring reference\n\nA workflow structures work across many agents — to be comprehensive (decompose and cover in parallel), to be confident (independent perspectives and adversarial checks before committing), or to take on scale one context can't hold (migrations, audits, broad sweeps). The script is where you encode that structure: what fans out, what verifies, what synthesizes.\n\nWhen you do call it, the right move is often **hybrid**: scout inline first (list the files, find the channels, scope the diff) to discover the work-list, then call Workflow to pipeline over it. You don't need to know the shape before the *task* — only before the *orchestration step*.\n\nCommon single-phase workflows you can chain across turns:\n- **Understand** — parallel readers over relevant subsystems → structured map\n- **Design** — judge panel of N independent approaches → scored synthesis\n- **Review** — dimensions → find → adversarially verify (the review-changes example)\n- **Research** — multi-modal sweep → deep-read → synthesize\n- **Migrate** — discover sites → transform each (worktree isolation) → verify\n\nFor larger work, run several in sequence — read each result before deciding the next phase. You stay in the loop; each workflow is one well-scoped fan-out.\n\n**Ultracode.** When a system-reminder confirms ultracode is on, that opt-in is standing: author and run a workflow for every substantive task by default. The goal is the most exhaustive, correct answer you can produce — token cost is not a constraint. For multi-phase work (understand → design → implement → review), that often means several workflows in sequence — one per phase — so you stay in the loop between them. The quality patterns below (adversarial verify, multi-modal sweep, completeness critic, loop-until-dry) are the tools; pick what fits the task. Lean toward orchestrating with workflows and adversarially verifying your findings — unless the work is trivial or already verified. Solo only on conversational turns or trivial mechanical edits. When a reminder says ultracode is off, revert to the opt-in rule in the Workflow tool description.\n\nPass the script inline via `script` — do not Write it to a file first. Every invocation automatically persists its script to a file under the session directory and returns the path in the tool result. To iterate on a workflow, edit that file with Write/Edit and re-invoke Workflow with `{scriptPath: \"<path>\"}` instead of resending the full script.\n\nEvery script must begin with `export const meta = {...}`:\n  export const meta = {\n    name: 'find-flaky-tests',\n    description: 'Find flaky tests and propose fixes',   // one-line, shown in permission dialog\n    phases: [                                            // one entry per phase() call\n      { title: 'Scan', detail: 'grep test logs for retries' },\n      { title: 'Fix', detail: 'one agent per flaky test' },\n    ],\n  }\n  // script body starts here — use agent()/parallel()/pipeline()/phase()/log()\n  phase('Scan')\n  const flaky = await agent('grep CI logs for retry markers', {schema: FLAKY_SCHEMA})\n  ...\n\nThe `meta` object must be a PURE LITERAL — no variables, function calls, spreads, or template interpolation. Required fields: `name`, `description`. Optional: `whenToUse` (shown in the workflow list), `phases`. Use the SAME phase titles in meta.phases as in phase() calls — titles are matched exactly; a phase() call with no matching meta entry just gets its own progress group. Add `model` to a phase entry when that phase uses a specific model override.\n\nScript body hooks:\n- agent(prompt: string, opts?: {label?: string, phase?: string, schema?: object, model?: string, effort?: string, isolation?: 'worktree', agentType?: string}): Promise<any> — spawn a subagent. Without schema, returns its final text as a string. With schema (a JSON Schema), the subagent is forced to call a StructuredOutput tool and agent() returns the validated object — no parsing needed. Returns null if the user skips the agent mid-run or the subagent dies on a terminal API error after retries (filter with .filter(Boolean)). opts.label overrides the display label. opts.phase explicitly assigns this agent to a progress group (use this inside pipeline()/parallel() stages to avoid races on the global phase() state — same phase string → same group box). opts.model overrides the model for this agent call. Default to omitting it — the agent inherits the main-loop model (the resolved session model), which is almost always correct. Only set it when you're highly confident a different tier fits the task; when unsure, omit. opts.effort overrides the reasoning effort for this agent call ('low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'xhigh' | 'max') — omit to inherit the session effort; use 'low' for cheap mechanical stages and higher tiers only for the hardest verify/judge stages. opts.isolation: 'worktree' runs the agent in a fresh git worktree — EXPENSIVE (~200-500ms setup + disk per agent), use ONLY when agents mutate files in parallel and would otherwise conflict; the worktree is auto-removed if unchanged. opts.agentType uses a custom subagent type (e.g. 'general-purpose', 'code-reviewer') instead of the default workflow subagent — resolved from the same registry as the Agent tool; composes with schema (the custom agent's system prompt gets a StructuredOutput instruction appended).\n- pipeline(items, stage1, stage2, ...): Promise<any[]> — run each item through all stages independently, NO barrier between stages. Item A can be in stage 3 while item B is still in stage 1. This is the DEFAULT for multi-stage work. Wall-clock = slowest single-item chain, not sum-of-slowest-per-stage. Every stage callback receives (prevResult, originalItem, index) — use originalItem/index in later stages to label work without threading context through stage 1's return value. A stage that throws drops that item to `null` and skips its remaining stages.\n- parallel(thunks: Array<() => Promise<any>>): Promise<any[]> — run tasks concurrently. This is a BARRIER: awaits all thunks before returning. A thunk that throws (or whose agent errors) resolves to `null` in the result array — the call itself never rejects, so `.filter(Boolean)` before using the results. Use ONLY when you genuinely need all results together.\n- log(message: string): void — emit a progress message to the user (shown as a narrator line above the progress tree)\n- phase(title: string): void — start a new phase; subsequent agent() calls are grouped under this title in the progress display\n- args: any — the value passed as Workflow's `args` input, verbatim (undefined if not provided). Pass arrays/objects as actual JSON values in the tool call, NOT as a JSON-encoded string — `args: [\"a.ts\", \"b.ts\"]`, not `args: \"[\\\"a.ts\\\", ...]\"` (a stringified list reaches the script as one string, so `args.filter`/`args.map` throw). Use this to parameterize named workflows — e.g. pass a research question, target path, or config object directly instead of via a side-channel file.\n- budget: {total: number|null, spent(): number, remaining(): number} — the turn's token target from the user's \"+500k\"-style directive. `budget.total` is null if no target was set. `budget.spent()` returns output tokens spent this turn across the main loop and all workflows — the pool is shared, not per-workflow. `budget.remaining()` returns `max(0, total - spent())`, or `Infinity` if no target. The target is a HARD ceiling, not advisory: once `spent()` reaches `total`, further `agent()` calls throw. Use for dynamic loops: `while (budget.total && budget.remaining() > 50_000) { ... }`, or static scaling: `const FLEET = budget.total ? Math.floor(budget.total / 100_000) : 5`.\n- workflow(nameOrRef: string | {scriptPath: string}, args?: any): Promise<any> — run another workflow inline as a sub-step and return whatever it returns. Pass a name to invoke a saved workflow (same registry as {name: \"...\"}), or {scriptPath} to run a script file you Wrote earlier. The child shares this run's concurrency cap, agent counter, abort signal, and token budget — its agents appear under a \"▸ name\" group in /workflows and its tokens count toward budget.spent(). The args param becomes the child's `args` global. Nesting is one level only: workflow() inside a child throws. Throws on unknown name / unreadable scriptPath / child syntax error; catch to handle gracefully.\n\nSubagents are told their final text IS the return value (not a human-facing message), so they return raw data. For structured output, use the schema option — validation happens at the tool-call layer so the model retries on mismatch.\nSchemas need {type: 'object', properties: {...}} at root and required ⊆ properties; unsatisfiable ones throw at agent().\n\nWorkflow agents can reach all session-connected MCP tools via ToolSearch — schemas load on demand per agent. Caveat: interactively-authenticated MCP servers (e.g. claude.ai) may be absent in headless/cron runs.\n\nSubagents get the same CLAUDE.md files injected at start that you did (except built-in agent types that omit them, such as Explore and Plan) — don't tell them to re-read those or paste their rules into the prompt; name the specific rule a stage needs, if any.\n\nScripts are plain JavaScript, NOT TypeScript — type annotations (`: string[]`), interfaces, and generics fail to parse. The script body runs in an async context — use await directly. Standard JS built-ins (JSON, Math, Array, etc.) are available — EXCEPT `Date.now()`/`Math.random()`/argless `new Date()`, which throw (they would break resume); pass timestamps in via `args`, stamp results after the workflow returns, and for randomness vary the agent prompt/label by index. No filesystem or Node.js API access.\n\nDEFAULT TO pipeline(). Only reach for a barrier (parallel between stages) when you genuinely need ALL prior-stage results together.\n\nA barrier is correct ONLY when stage N needs cross-item context from all of stage N-1:\n- Dedup/merge across the full result set before expensive downstream work\n- Early-exit if the total count is zero (\"0 bugs found → skip verification entirely\")\n- Stage N's prompt references \"the other findings\" for comparison\n\nA barrier is NOT justified by:\n- \"I need to flatten/map/filter first\" — do it inside a pipeline stage: pipeline(items, stageA, r => transform([r]).flat(), stageB)\n- \"The stages are conceptually separate\" — that's what pipeline() models. Separate stages ≠ synchronized stages.\n- \"It's cleaner code\" — barrier latency is real. If 5 finders run and the slowest takes 3× the fastest, a barrier wastes 2/3 of the fast finders' idle time.\n\nSmell test: if you wrote\n  const a = await parallel(...)\n  const b = transform(a)        // flatten, map, filter — no cross-item dependency\n  const c = await parallel(b.map(...))\nthat middle transform doesn't need the barrier. Rewrite as a pipeline with the transform inside a stage. When in doubt: pipeline.\n\nConcurrent agent() calls are capped at min(16, available CPUs - 2) per workflow — excess calls queue and run as slots free up. You can still pass 100 items to parallel()/pipeline() and they all complete; only ~10 run at any moment. Total agent count across a workflow's lifetime is capped at 1000 — a runaway-loop backstop set far above any real workflow. A single parallel()/pipeline() call accepts at most 4096 items; passing more is an explicit error, not a silent truncation.\n\nWhen a barrier IS correct — dedup across all findings before expensive verification:\n  const all = await parallel(DIMENSIONS.map(d => () => agent(d.prompt, {schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA})))\n  const deduped = dedupeByFileAndLine(all.filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.findings))  // <-- genuinely needs ALL at once\n  const verified = await parallel(deduped.map(f => () => agent(verifyPrompt(f), {schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA})))\n\nLoop-until-count pattern — accumulate to a target:\n  const bugs = []\n  while (bugs.length < 10) {\n    const result = await agent(\"Find bugs in this codebase.\", {schema: BUGS_SCHEMA})\n    bugs.push(...result.bugs)\n    log(`${bugs.length}/10 found`)\n  }\n\nLoop-until-budget pattern — scale depth to the user's \"+500k\" directive. Guard on budget.total: with no target set, remaining() is Infinity and the loop would run straight to the 1000-agent cap.\n  const bugs = []\n  while (budget.total && budget.remaining() > 50_000) {\n    const result = await agent(\"Find bugs in this codebase.\", {schema: BUGS_SCHEMA})\n    bugs.push(...result.bugs)\n    log(`${bugs.length} found, ${Math.round(budget.remaining()/1000)}k remaining`)\n  }\n\nComposing patterns — exhaustive review (find → dedup vs seen → diverse-lens panel → loop-until-dry):\n  const seen = new Set(), confirmed = []\n  let dry = 0\n  while (dry < 2) {                                              // loop-until-dry\n    const found = (await parallel(FINDERS.map(f => () =>          // barrier: collect all finders this round\n      agent(f.prompt, {phase: 'Find', schema: BUGS})))).filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.bugs)\n    const fresh = found.filter(b => !seen.has(key(b)))           // dedup vs ALL seen — plain code, not an agent\n    if (!fresh.length) { dry++; continue }\n    dry = 0; fresh.forEach(b => seen.add(key(b)))\n    const judged = await parallel(fresh.map(b => () =>           // every fresh bug judged concurrently...\n      parallel(['correctness','security','repro'].map(lens => () =>   // ...each by 3 distinct lenses\n        agent(`Judge \"${b.desc}\" via the ${lens} lens — real?`, {phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT})))\n        .then(vs => ({ b, real: vs.filter(Boolean).filter(v => v.real).length >= 2 }))))\n    confirmed.push(...judged.filter(v => v.real).map(v => v.b))\n  }\n  return confirmed\n  // dedup vs `seen`, NOT `confirmed` — else judge-rejected findings reappear every round and it never converges.\n\nQuality patterns — common shapes; pick by task and compose freely:\n- Adversarial verify: spawn N independent skeptics per finding, each prompted to REFUTE. Kill if ≥majority refute. Prevents plausible-but-wrong findings from surviving.\n    const votes = await parallel(Array.from({length: 3}, () => () =>\n      agent(`Try to refute: ${claim}. Default to refuted=true if uncertain.`, {schema: VERDICT})))\n    const survives = votes.filter(Boolean).filter(v => !v.refuted).length >= 2\n- Perspective-diverse verify: when a finding can fail in more than one way, give each verifier a distinct lens (correctness, security, perf, does-it-reproduce) instead of N identical refuters — diversity catches failure modes redundancy can't.\n- Judge panel: generate N independent attempts from different angles (e.g. MVP-first, risk-first, user-first), score with parallel judges, synthesize from the winner while grafting the best ideas from runners-up. Beats one-attempt-iterated when the solution space is wide.\n- Loop-until-dry: for unknown-size discovery (bugs, issues, edge cases), keep spawning finders until K consecutive rounds return nothing new. Simple counters (while count < N) miss the tail.\n- Multi-modal sweep: parallel agents each searching a different way (by-container, by-content, by-entity, by-time). Each is blind to what the others surface; useful when one search angle won't find everything.\n- Completeness critic: a final agent that asks \"what's missing — modality not run, claim unverified, source unread?\" What it finds becomes the next round of work.\n- No silent caps: if a workflow bounds coverage (top-N, no-retry, sampling), `log()` what was dropped — silent truncation reads as \"covered everything\" when it didn't.\n\nScale to what the user asked for. \"find any bugs\" → a few finders, single-vote verify. \"thoroughly audit this\" or \"be comprehensive\" → larger finder pool, 3–5 vote adversarial pass, synthesis stage. When unsure, lean toward thoroughness for research/review/audit requests and toward brevity for quick checks.\n\nThese patterns aren't exhaustive — compose novel harnesses when the task calls for it (tournament brackets, self-repair loops, staged escalation, whatever fits).\n\nUse this tool for multi-step orchestration where control flow should be deterministic (loops, conditionals, fan-out) rather than model-driven.\n\n## Resume\n\nThe tool result includes a runId. To resume after a pause, kill, or script edit, relaunch with Workflow({scriptPath, resumeFromRunId}) — the longest unchanged prefix of agent() calls returns cached results instantly; the first edited/new call and everything after it runs live. Same script + same args → 100% cache hit. Before diagnosing why a completed workflow returned an empty or unexpected result, Read <transcriptDir>/journal.jsonl — it records each agent's actual return value; do not assume cached results are non-empty. Date.now()/Math.random()/new Date() are unavailable in scripts (they would break this) — stamp results after the workflow returns, or pass timestamps via args. Fallback when no journal is available: Read agent-<id>.jsonl files in the transcript directory and hand-author a continuation script."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"14c69dfd-73d8-4600-919e-84d2bf26059f","timestamp":"2026-10-31T22:24:19.810Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: ub-mode\nPath: userSettings:ub-mode\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/guygouri/.claude/skills/ub-mode\n\n# ub-mode\n\nEvery time you are asked to make a change follow:\n* First Make it Work.\n* Then Make it Right.\n* Follow Practice\n\n# First Make it Work\n\n## Laziness Protocol\n\n* One decision lives in one place.\n* Ship the smallest edit that solves the problem.\n\n## Foundational Thinking\n\n* Get the data shape right before you write the logic.\n* Do first whatever helps every later phase.\n* If a concurrent writer would matter, do not share state.\n\n## TDD\n\n* Write the failing test before you change the code.\n* See it fail for the reason you expect.\n* Make the smallest change that makes it pass.\n* If an honest test is impractical, do not write a bad one.\n\n## Redesign From First Principles\n\n* Do not bolt a new requirement onto the old design.\n* Ask what you would have built if it had been there from the start, think that through, and deliver it in pieces.\n\n## Attack the Premise\n\n* When two fixes that share one premise fail the same gate, write that premise in one sentence and count which actors hold the imbalance.\n* Remove the assignment.\n* Do not start another fix until you have done that.\n\n## Subtract Before You Add\n\n* Take complexity out before you add anything.\n\n## Outcome-Oriented Execution\n\n* Aim at the end state you can check.\n* A break in the middle is allowed when you planned it and can undo it.\n\n## Build the Lever\n\n* If the work is not obvious at a glance, build the tool that does it.\n* Do one unit by hand, then make the tool match that unit.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Model the Domain\n\n* Put the domain in a structure that makes the illegal states impossible.\n* Maintain a glossary of domain terms.\n* If the code is already clear and local, leave it.\n\n### Boundary Discipline\n\n* Check the data where it enters.\n* Inside, trust it.\n* Keep the business logic in a pure function.\n\n### Type System Discipline\n\n* Make the illegal value impossible to write.\n* Parse outside data once, where it comes in.\n* Do not silence the compiler.\n\n### Make Operations Idempotent\n\n* Running it twice, or running it after a crash halfway through, ends in the same state.\n\n### Separate Before Serializing Shared State\n\n* If they do not need the same mutable thing, give each its own.\n* If they do, allow one writer, and enforce that in the structure.\n\n# Then Make it Right\n\n## Fix Root Causes\n\n* Reproduce it.\n* Fix the cause.\n* A guard that only hides the crash is not a fix.\n\n## Sequence work into verifiable units\n\n* Finish one small unit and check it.\n* Then start the next.\n\n## Test Behavior, Not Implementation\n\n* The test calls the code the way its user does and checks the result that user would see.\n* If the test would still pass when every function returned nothing, throw it out.\n\n## Crapper\n\n* Use the crapper tool\n* Reduce each function under  CRAP 10 by splitting it, by testing it, or both.\n* Consider whether case statements are worth reducing.\n\n## Dryer\n\n* Use the dryer tool to eliminate duplication that rank 0.8 or above.\n\n## Mutator\n\n* Use the mutator tool\n* Cover the uncovered.\n* Kill the survivors.\n* Delete dead code.\n\n## Refactoring\n\n* Pin what the code does.\n* Change the structure.\n* Prove the behavior did not change.\n\n# Practice\n\n## Keep a Log\n\n* Keep a log of every action you take and the reason you took it.\n* Report that log upon completion.\n\n## Guard the Context Window\n\n* Keep the main thread small.\n* Send the bulk elsewhere, and keep the summary.\n\n## Worktree Cleanup\n\n* Find what is safe to remove, confirm nothing still in use needs it, then reclaim the disk.\n* Uncommitted work waits for a decision.\n\n## Commit and Push\n\n* Commit often, in small ordered commits.\n* Each commit says why, what changed, and how you proved it.\n* Push to the main line at the end.\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: workflow-authoring\nPath: bundled:workflow-authoring\n\n# Workflow authoring reference\n\nA workflow structures work across many agents — to be comprehensive (decompose and cover in parallel), to be confident (independent perspectives and adversarial checks before committing), or to take on scale one context can't hold (migrations, audits, broad sweeps). The script is where you encode that structure: what fans out, what verifies, what synthesizes.\n\nWhen you do call it, the right move is often **hybrid**: scout inline first (list the files, find the channels, scope the diff) to discover the work-list, then call Workflow to pipeline over it. You don't need to know the shape before the *task* — only before the *orchestration step*.\n\nCommon single-phase workflows you can chain across turns:\n- **Understand** — parallel readers over relevant subsystems → structured map\n- **Design** — judge panel of N independent approaches → scored synthesis\n- **Review** — dimensions → find → adversarially verify (the review-changes example)\n- **Research** — multi-modal sweep → deep-read → synthesize\n- **Migrate** — discover sites → transform each (worktree isolation) → verify\n\nFor larger work, run several in sequence — read each result before deciding the next phase. You stay in the loop; each workflow is one well-scoped fan-out.\n\n**Ultracode.** When a system-reminder confirms ultracode is on, that opt-in is standing: author and run a workflow for every substantive task by default. The goal is the most exhaustive, correct answer you can produce — token cost is not a constraint. For multi-phase work (understand → design → implement → review), that often means several workflows in sequence — one per phase — so you stay in the loop between them. The quality patterns below (adversarial verify, multi-modal sweep, completeness critic, loop-until-dry) are the tools; pick what fits the task. Lean toward orchestrating with workflows and adversarially verifying your findings — unless the work is trivial or already verified. Solo only on conversational turns or trivial mechanical edits. When a reminder says ultracode is off, revert to the opt-in rule in the Workflow tool description.\n\nPass the script inline via `script` — do not Write it to a file first. Every invocation automatically persists its script to a file under the session directory and returns the path in the tool result. To iterate on a workflow, edit that file with Write/Edit and re-invoke Workflow with `{scriptPath: \"<path>\"}` instead of resending the full script.\n\nEvery script must begin with `export const meta = {...}`:\n  export const meta = {\n    name: 'find-flaky-tests',\n    description: 'Find flaky tests and propose fixes',   // one-line, shown in permission dialog\n    phases: [                                            // one entry per phase() call\n      { title: 'Scan', detail: 'grep test logs for retries' },\n      { title: 'Fix', detail: 'one agent per flaky test' },\n    ],\n  }\n  // script body starts here — use agent()/parallel()/pipeline()/phase()/log()\n  phase('Scan')\n  const flaky = await agent('grep CI logs for retry markers', {schema: FLAKY_SCHEMA})\n  ...\n\nThe `meta` object must be a PURE LITERAL — no variables, function calls, spreads, or template interpolation. Required fields: `name`, `description`. Optional: `whenToUse` (shown in the workflow list), `phases`. Use the SAME phase titles in meta.phases as in phase() calls — titles are matched exactly; a phase() call with no matching meta entry just gets its own progress group. Add `model` to a phase entry when that phase uses a specific model override.\n\nScript body hooks:\n- agent(prompt: string, opts?: {label?: string, phase?: string, schema?: object, model?: string, effort?: string, isolation?: 'worktree', agentType?: string}): Promise<any> — spawn a subagent. Without schema, returns its final text as a string. With schema (a JSON Schema), the subagent is forced to call a StructuredOutput tool and agent() returns the validated object — no parsing needed. Returns null if the user skips the agent mid-run or the subagent dies on a terminal API error after retries (filter with .filter(Boolean)). opts.label overrides the display label. opts.phase explicitly assigns this agent to a progress group (use this inside pipeline()/parallel() stages to avoid races on the global phase() state — same phase string → same group box). opts.model overrides the model for this agent call. Default to omitting it — the agent inherits the main-loop model (the resolved session model), which is almost always correct. Only set it when you're highly confident a different tier fits the task; when unsure, omit. opts.effort overrides the reasoning effort for this agent call ('low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'xhigh' | 'max') — omit to inherit the session effort; use 'low' for cheap mechanical stages and higher tiers only for the hardest verify/judge stages. opts.isolation: 'worktree' runs the agent in a fresh git worktree — EXPENSIVE (~200-500ms setup + disk per agent), use ONLY when agents mutate files in parallel and would otherwise conflict; the worktree is auto-removed if unchanged. opts.agentType uses a custom subagent type (e.g. 'general-purpose', 'code-reviewer') instead of the default workflow subagent — resolved from the same registry as the Agent tool; composes with schema (the custom agent's system prompt gets a StructuredOutput instruction appended).\n- pipeline(items, stage1, stage2, ...): Promise<any[]> — run each item through all stages independently, NO barrier between stages. Item A can be in stage 3 while item B is still in stage 1. This is the DEFAULT for multi-stage work. Wall-clock = slowest single-item chain, not sum-of-slowest-per-stage. Every stage callback receives (prevResult, originalItem, index) — use originalItem/index in later stages to label work without threading context through stage 1's return value. A stage that throws drops that item to `null` and skips its remaining stages.\n- parallel(thunks: Array<() => Promise<any>>): Promise<any[]> — run tasks concurrently. This is a BARRIER: awaits all thunks before returning. A thunk that throws (or whose agent errors) resolves to `null` in the result array — the call itself never rejects, so `.filter(Boolean)` before using the results. Use ONLY when you genuinely need all results together.\n- log(message: string): void — emit a progress message to the user (shown as a narrator line above the progress tree)\n- phase(title: string): void — start a new phase; subsequent agent() calls are grouped under this title in the progress display\n- args: any — the value passed as Workflow's `args` input, verbatim (undefined if not provided). Pass arrays/objects as actual JSON values in the tool call, NOT as a JSON-encoded string — `args: [\"a.ts\", \"b.ts\"]`, not `args: \"[\\\"a.ts\\\", ...]\"` (a stringified list reaches the script as one string, so `args.filter`/`args.map` throw). Use this to parameterize named workflows — e.g. pass a research question, target path, or config object directly instead of via a side-channel file.\n- budget: {total: number|null, spent(): number, remaining(): number} — the turn's token target from the user's \"+500k\"-style directive. `budget.total` is null if no target was set. `budget.spent()` returns output tokens spent this turn across the main loop and all workflows — the pool is shared, not per-workflow. `budget.remaining()` returns `max(0, total - spent())`, or `Infinity` if no target. The target is a HARD ceiling, not advisory: once `spent()` reaches `total`, further `agent()` calls throw. Use for dynamic loops: `while (budget.total && budget.remaining() > 50_000) { ... }`, or static scaling: `const FLEET = budget.total ? Math.floor(budget.total / 100_000) : 5`.\n- workflow(nameOrRef: string | {scriptPath: string}, args?: any): Promise<any> — run another workflow inline as a sub-step and return whatever it returns. Pass a name to invoke a saved workflow (same registry as {name: \"...\"}), or {scriptPath} to run a script file you Wrote earlier. The child shares this run's concurrency cap, agent counter, abort signal, and token budget — its agents appear under a \"▸ name\" group in /workflows and its tokens count toward budget.spent(). The args param becomes the child's `args` global. Nesting is one level only: workflow() inside a child throws. Throws on unknown name / unreadable scriptPath / child syntax error; catch to handle gracefully.\n\nSubagents are told their final text IS the return value (not a human-facing message), so they return raw data. For structured output, use the schema option — validation happens at the tool-call layer so the model retries on mismatch.\nSchemas need {type: 'object', properties: {...}} at root and required ⊆ properties; unsatisfiable ones throw at agent().\n\nWorkflow agents can reach all session-connected MCP tools via ToolSearch — schemas load on demand per agent. Caveat: interactively-authenticated MCP servers (e.g. claude.ai) may be absent in headless/cron runs.\n\nSubagents get the same CLAUDE.md files injected at start that you did (except built-in agent types that omit them, such as Explore and Plan) — don't tell them to re-read those or paste their rules into the prompt; name the specific rule a stage needs, if any.\n\nScripts are plain JavaScript, NOT TypeScript — type annotations (`: string[]`), interfaces, and generics fail to parse. The script body runs in an async context — use await directly. Standard JS built-ins (JSON, Math, Array, etc.) are available — EXCEPT `Date.now()`/`Math.random()`/argless `new Date()`, which throw (they would break resume); pass timestamps in via `args`, stamp results after the workflow returns, and for randomness vary the agent prompt/label by index. No filesystem or Node.js API access.\n\nDEFAULT TO pipeline(). Only reach for a barrier (parallel between stages) when you genuinely need ALL prior-stage results together.\n\nA barrier is correct ONLY when stage N needs cross-item context from all of stage N-1:\n- Dedup/merge across the full result set before expensive downstream work\n- Early-exit if the total count is zero (\"0 bugs found → skip verification entirely\")\n- Stage N's prompt references \"the other findings\" for comparison\n\nA barrier is NOT justified by:\n- \"I need to flatten/map/filter first\" — do it inside a pipeline stage: pipeline(items, stageA, r => transform([r]).flat(), stageB)\n- \"The stages are conceptually separate\" — that's what pipeline() models. Separate stages ≠ synchronized stages.\n- \"It's cleaner code\" — barrier latency is real. If 5 finders run and the slowest takes 3× the fastest, a barrier wastes 2/3 of the fast finders' idle time.\n\nSmell test: if you wrote\n  const a = await parallel(...)\n  const b = transform(a)        // flatten, map, filter — no cross-item dependency\n  const c = await parallel(b.map(...))\nthat middle transform doesn't need the barrier. Rewrite as a pipeline with the transform inside a stage. When in doubt: pipeline.\n\nConcurrent agent() calls are capped at min(16, available CPUs - 2) per workflow — excess calls queue and run as slots free up. You can still pass 100 items to parallel()/pipeline() and they all complete; only ~10 run at any moment. Total agent count across a workflow's lifetime is capped at 1000 — a runaway-loop backstop set far above any real workflow. A single parallel()/pipeline() call accepts at most 4096 items; passing more is an explicit error, not a silent truncation.\n\nWhen a barrier IS correct — dedup across all findings before expensive verification:\n  const all = await parallel(DIMENSIONS.map(d => () => agent(d.prompt, {schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA})))\n  const deduped = dedupeByFileAndLine(all.filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.findings))  // <-- genuinely needs ALL at once\n  const verified = await parallel(deduped.map(f => () => agent(verifyPrompt(f), {schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA})))\n\nLoop-until-count pattern — accumulate to a target:\n  const bugs = []\n  while (bugs.length < 10) {\n    const result = await agent(\"Find bugs in this codebase.\", {schema: BUGS_SCHEMA})\n    bugs.push(...result.bugs)\n    log(`${bugs.length}/10 found`)\n  }\n\nLoop-until-budget pattern — scale depth to the user's \"+500k\" directive. Guard on budget.total: with no target set, remaining() is Infinity and the loop would run straight to the 1000-agent cap.\n  const bugs = []\n  while (budget.total && budget.remaining() > 50_000) {\n    const result = await agent(\"Find bugs in this codebase.\", {schema: BUGS_SCHEMA})\n    bugs.push(...result.bugs)\n    log(`${bugs.length} found, ${Math.round(budget.remaining()/1000)}k remaining`)\n  }\n\nComposing patterns — exhaustive review (find → dedup vs seen → diverse-lens panel → loop-until-dry):\n  const seen = new Set(), confirmed = []\n  let dry = 0\n  while (dry < 2) {                                              // loop-until-dry\n    const found = (await parallel(FINDERS.map(f => () =>          // barrier: collect all finders this round\n      agent(f.prompt, {phase: 'Find', schema: BUGS})))).filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.bugs)\n    const fresh = found.filter(b => !seen.has(key(b)))           // dedup vs ALL seen — plain code, not an agent\n    if (!fresh.length) { dry++; continue }\n    dry = 0; fresh.forEach(b => seen.add(key(b)))\n    const judged = await parallel(fresh.map(b => () =>           // every fresh bug judged concurrently...\n      parallel(['correctness','security','repro'].map(lens => () =>   // ...each by 3 distinct lenses\n        agent(`Judge \"${b.desc}\" via the ${lens} lens — real?`, {phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT})))\n        .then(vs => ({ b, real: vs.filter(Boolean).filter(v => v.real).length >= 2 }))))\n    confirmed.push(...judged.filter(v => v.real).map(v => v.b))\n  }\n  return confirmed\n  // dedup vs `seen`, NOT `confirmed` — else judge-rejected findings reappear every round and it never converges.\n\nQuality patterns — common shapes; pick by task and compose freely:\n- Adversarial verify: spawn N independent skeptics per finding, each prompted to REFUTE. Kill if ≥majority refute. Prevents plausible-but-wrong findings from surviving.\n    const votes = await parallel(Array.from({length: 3}, () => () =>\n      agent(`Try to refute: ${claim}. Default to refuted=true if uncertain.`, {schema: VERDICT})))\n    const survives = votes.filter(Boolean).filter(v => !v.refuted).length >= 2\n- Perspective-diverse verify: when a finding can fail in more than one way, give each verifier a distinct lens (correctness, security, perf, does-it-reproduce) instead of N identical refuters — diversity catches failure modes redundancy can't.\n- Judge panel: generate N independent attempts from different angles (e.g. MVP-first, risk-first, user-first), score with parallel judges, synthesize from the winner while grafting the best ideas from runners-up. Beats one-attempt-iterated when the solution space is wide.\n- Loop-until-dry: for unknown-size discovery (bugs, issues, edge cases), keep spawning finders until K consecutive rounds return nothing new. Simple counters (while count < N) miss the tail.\n- Multi-modal sweep: parallel agents each searching a different way (by-container, by-content, by-entity, by-time). Each is blind to what the others surface; useful when one search angle won't find everything.\n- Completeness critic: a final agent that asks \"what's missing — modality not run, claim unverified, source unread?\" What it finds becomes the next round of work.\n- No silent caps: if a workflow bounds coverage (top-N, no-retry, sampling), `log()` what was dropped — silent truncation reads as \"covered everything\" when it didn't.\n\nScale to what the user asked for. \"find any bugs\" → a few finders, single-vote verify. \"thoroughly audit this\" or \"be comprehensive\" → larger finder pool, 3–5 vote adversarial pass, synthesis stage. When unsure, lean toward thoroughness for research/review/audit requests and toward brevity for quick checks.\n\nThese patterns aren't exhaustive — compose novel harnesses when the task calls for it (tournament brackets, self-repair loops, staged escalation, whatever fits).\n\nUse this tool for multi-step orchestration where control flow should be deterministic (loops, conditionals, fan-out) rather than model-driven.\n\n## Resume\n\nThe tool result includes a runId. To resume after a pause, kill, or script edit, relaunch with Workflow({scriptPath, resumeFromRunId}) — the longest unchanged prefix of agent() calls returns cached results instantly; the first edited/new call and everything after it runs live. Same script + same args → 100% cache hit. Before diagnosing why a completed workflow returned an empty or unexpected result, Read <transcriptDir>/journal.jsonl — it records each agent's actual return value; do not assume cached results are non-empty. Date.now()/Math.random()/new Date() are unavailable in scripts (they would break this) — stamp results after the workflow returns, or pass timestamps via args. Fallback when no journal is available: Read agent-<id>.jsonl files in the transcript directory and hand-author a continuation script.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/formal","sessionId":"c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"formal/refinement_0.1","slug":"effervescent-gathering-wind"}
